# G2 GPU timing stability on a LOADED box — six runs of one binary, one input
#
# instrument   sigma.cu (this directory), compiled nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_89
# input        ooo.bin, sha256 9bd4d3523fc5b8a4397bcd0e93583fb8b2e1f33c7b1122058f1790650c0f1710
# invocation   ./sigma ooo.bin 20        (20 reps per timed block)
# host         i9-13900HX + RTX 4090 Laptop, loadavg 61-72 across the series
# device       idle before each run (nvidia-smi 0%, 15 MiB)
# reps         NOT pinned; no taskset. That is the point of the reading.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the banked record carried 65.7 (kernel only) and 69.8 (with host
# round trip) -- the round trip reported FASTER than the kernel it contains. Two
# diagnoses were tried and both were wrong (swapped labels; 69.8 unsourceable). This
# series is what settled it.

run   kernel_only_ms   sigma/s     round_trip_ms   sigma/s
  1        14.820        67.5          15.142       66.0
  2        18.168        55.0          89.822       11.1
  3        17.419        57.4          14.903       67.1
  4        17.686        56.5          80.959       12.4
  5        17.007        58.8          81.067       12.3
  6        14.867        67.3          14.904       67.1

kernel only    14.820 - 18.168 ms   = 1.23x spread
round trip     BIMODAL: 3 runs ~14.9 ms, 3 runs 81-90 ms = 6.03x spread
slow-mode excess over fast mode   66.1 ms
measured PCIe round trip (probe.cu, 1.58 MiB CI vector)   0.50 ms
                                  ratio   132x

READ THIS AS: no device-side mechanism moves 1.58 MiB 132x slower than its measured
PCIe cost. The slow mode is the HOST THREAD DESCHEDULED at loadavg 72. The same cause
inflates the kernel-only block, because cudaEventElapsedTime over 20 back-to-back reps
charges host gaps to the device clock whenever the host cannot keep the launch queue fed.

CONSEQUENCE: a single GPU timing on this box is a DRAW, not a measurement, and the
ordering between two independently contaminated blocks carries no information. The banked
65.7 and 69.8 are two such draws. Every timing row in the G2 record should have carried a
spread beside it -- RESOURCE_DESIGN section 9 Q4 requires exactly that of a REGISTRY entry,
and the benchmark record did not hold itself to its own crate's rule.

CORROBORATION, same day, independent instrument: gpu-production's fci_bench (holon-gpu
production path, taskset-pinned, own launch header) reported kernel only 58.9 / with round
trip 65.2 -- the same impossible ordering -- while measuring the GPU rate over five timing
runs at 69.40 +/- 1.01, and its registry spot-check convicted its own 58.9 against that
mean. See conformance/atomworld/gpu_fci/.

STILL OWED: the quiet-window measurement, on BOTH arms. The GPU arm's exposure is not
smaller than the CPU arm's, so the direction of the contention bias in the 3.2x ratio is
not established. M-PLACEMENT-LOTTERY third rung.


================================================================================
SERIES 2 — 200 reps per block, and the diagnosis above is SUPERSEDED
================================================================================
# Run after gpu-prod supplied a rival mechanism and a 12-invocation series. Their
# hypothesis predicted my kernel spread was an artifact of my own instrument. It is.

run   kernel_only_ms   sigma/s     round_trip_ms   sigma/s      loadavg 66-70
  1        14.618        68.4          34.581       28.9
  2        14.604        68.5          33.597       29.8
  3        14.755        67.8          17.581       56.9

  kernel only, 200 reps    67.8 - 68.5 sigma/s     spread 1.010x   mean 68.22
  kernel only,  20 reps    55.0 - 67.5 sigma/s     spread 1.23x    (series 1)
  round trip,  200 reps    28.9 - 56.9 sigma/s     spread 1.97x

CROSS-INSTRUMENT AGREEMENT, which is what makes this trustworthy rather than another
single-lane story. gpu-prod ran twelve SEPARATE invocations of a DIFFERENT instrument
(holon-gpu fci_bench, one host wall-clock span over N launches, declared warm-up discard,
pinned to cpu0) across loadavg 67.5-86.6:

  their kernel-only   mean 68.253   sd 0.366 (0.54%)   spread 1.019x
  my    kernel-only   mean 68.22    over 3 runs        spread 1.010x

Two instruments, two timing methods, agreeing to 0.05% on the mean.

WHAT THIS OVERTURNS FROM SERIES 1:

1. THE KERNEL BLOCK IS STABLE. The 1.23x I measured was a WARM-UP TRANSIENT, not the
   kernel and not the host. At 20 reps a cold-clock ramp is ~1/10 of the timed span; at
   200 reps it is amortised away and the spread collapses to 1.01x. gpu-prod predicted
   this from their own instrument before I ran it.

2. THE INVERSION IS A COLD-CLOCK ARTIFACT, NOT A HOST STALL. sigma.cu times the
   kernel-only block FIRST and the round-trip block SECOND, so the first block pays the
   ramp. The arithmetic closes exactly:

       banked kernel-only   65.7 sigma/s = 15.221 ms    SLOWER than warm (14.60-14.76)
       banked round trip    69.8 sigma/s = 14.327 ms    at warm speed, host not stalling
       warm kernel measured today                        14.60-14.76 ms

   A partially-cold first block and a warm second block invert the ordering with no host
   stall required. gpu-prod saw the identical signature on their own first run
   (kernel 52.6 vs round trip 62.3) and fixed it with a declared 3-loop warm-up discard,
   after which their ordering has been correct in every run.

3. SO THE BANKED 65.7 UNDERSTATES THE KERNEL BY 3.7%. The honest warm kernel-only rate is
   68.2, not 65.7. The GPU arm was quoted BELOW its own rate.

WHAT SURVIVES FROM SERIES 1, and it is the round trip alone:

   The round-trip block IS genuinely host-exposed, and more reps make it WORSE, which is
   the signature of per-rep host synchronisation rather than of a ramp: 1.97x at 200 reps
   against 1.23x-with-bimodality at 20. Each rep does htod + dtoh with pageable memory, so
   each rep is a chance for a descheduled host to be charged. The 66.1 ms excess against a
   0.50 ms measured PCIe round trip (132x) stands as host descheduling; no device-side
   mechanism produces it.

TWO MECHANISMS, NOT ONE. Series 1 conflated them and attributed the inversion to the
host. Cold-clock ramp explains the INVERSION; host descheduling explains the ROUND TRIP's
instability. Both are real; they are not the same phenomenon and they act on different
blocks.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS ACCOUNT: a warm-started run (declared discard, no ramp) that still
inverts, which would put the inversion back on the host; or a 200-rep kernel block that
goes bimodal at higher load, which would mean the kernel block is host-exposed after all
and the amortisation only hid it. Neither is observed up to loadavg 87 across two
instruments.

HONEST NOTE ON THIS FILE'S OWN HISTORY: this is the FOURTH account of the same two
numbers, after (i) swapped labels, (ii) an unsourceable 69.8, (iii) two draws from one
contaminated distribution. The first three were each built on evidence I had and none
survived contact with more of it. The difference here is that the account is
cross-validated by an instrument I did not write, and it makes a falsifiable prediction
that has been checked. That is the only reason to trust it more than its predecessors.
